<python>
cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

A `pythonic` check function decides how much credit a submitted Python value
should receive.  It can also return feedback explaining the result.  This is
useful when equality alone is not the right policy: an answer might need a
particular type, satisfy several constraints, be numerically close, or earn
partial credit.

Check functions run after CAT-SOOP has parsed and evaluated the student's
expression in the configured sandbox.  They receive Python values, not the
student's original source text.

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>The Check Function Interface</catsoop-section>

Set `csq_check_function` to a function with two arguments:

```python
def csq_check_function(submission, solution):
    ...
```

`submission` is the value produced by the student's expression, and
`solution` is `csq_soln`.  For example:

<pre><code class="language-python">&lt;question pythonic&gt;
csq_name = "positive_even_integer"
csq_prompt = "Enter a positive, even integer:"
csq_soln = 2

def csq_check_function(submission, solution):
    return (
        isinstance(submission, int)
        and not isinstance(submission, bool)
        and submission &gt; 0
        and submission % 2 == 0
    )
&lt;/question&gt;</code></pre>

The checker does not have to use `solution`.  The two-argument signature is
still required because CAT-SOOP always calls the function as:

```python
csq_check_function(submission, csq_soln)
```

The default `pythonic` checker requires the values to have the same type and
compare equal:

```python
type(submission) == type(solution) and submission == solution
```

This type check matters in Python because some differently typed values
compare equal; for example, `True == 1`.

<catsoop-section>Scores and Messages</catsoop-section>

A custom checker may return any of these forms:

```python
True                         # full credit
False                        # no credit
0.6                          # 60 percent credit
(0.6, "Nearly there.")       # score and message
{"score": 0.6, "msg": "..."} # the same result as a mapping
```

The score is converted to a floating-point number and is normally between
`0` and `1`.  CAT-SOOP multiplies it by `csq_npoints`.  Thus a score of `0.6`
on a five-point question contributes three points.

A message supplied in the pair or mapping is displayed below the question
after the submission is processed.  If the checker returns only a scalar
score, `csq_msg_function(submission, solution)` can provide the message, but
returning the score and message together is usually easier to follow.

<catsoop-subsection>Targeted plain-text feedback</catsoop-subsection>

Checking one condition at a time lets the message tell the student what to
fix:

<pre><code class="language-python">&lt;question pythonic&gt;
csq_name = "five_probabilities"
csq_prompt = "Enter five distinct probabilities as a list:"
csq_soln = [0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0]
csq_npoints = 4

def csq_check_function(submission, solution):
    if not isinstance(submission, list):
        return 0, "Enter a Python list."
    if len(submission) != 5:
        return 0.25, "The list must contain exactly five values."
    if not all(isinstance(x, (int, float)) for x in submission):
        return 0.25, "Every item must be a number."
    if not all(0 &lt;= x &lt;= 1 for x in submission):
        return 0.5, "Every number must be between 0 and 1."
    if len(set(submission)) != len(submission):
        return 0.75, "The five probabilities must be distinct."
    return 1, "All constraints are satisfied."
&lt;/question&gt;</code></pre>

Here the messages describe the first failed condition, and the scores
implement an explicit partial-credit policy.

<catsoop-subsection>HTML feedback</catsoop-subsection>

The message is processed as HTML, not restricted to plain text.  It can
contain headings, lists, tables, links, code, math tags, and styled callouts:

<pre><code class="language-python">&lt;question pythonic&gt;
csq_name = "coordinate"
csq_prompt = "Enter the point (3, 4) as a tuple:"
csq_soln = (3, 4)

def csq_check_function(submission, solution):
    if not isinstance(submission, tuple):
        return {
            "score": 0,
            "msg": (
                '&lt;div class="bs-callout bs-callout-warning"&gt;'
                "&lt;b&gt;Use a tuple.&lt;/b&gt; "
                "Write the point as &lt;code&gt;(x, y)&lt;/code&gt;."
                "&lt;/div&gt;"
            ),
        }
    if submission != solution:
        return 0, (
            "&lt;p&gt;The required point has:&lt;/p&gt;"
            "&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;x-coordinate 3&lt;/li&gt;"
            "&lt;li&gt;y-coordinate 4&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;"
        )
    return 1, "&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Correct.&lt;/b&gt; You entered the expected point.&lt;/p&gt;"
&lt;/question&gt;</code></pre>

CAT-SOOP processes its supported custom HTML tags in the message as well.  For
example, a checker can use `<math>` or `<displaymath>` when feedback needs
typeset mathematics.

Messages are trusted course-author HTML.  Do not interpolate a student value
directly into them.  Escape it first:

```python
import html

safe_submission = html.escape(repr(submission))
message = "<p>Your value was <code>%s</code>.</p>" % safe_submission
```

Without escaping, text in the submitted value could become active markup in
the student's browser.

<catsoop-subsection>Images in feedback</catsoop-subsection>

An HTML message can include an image:

<pre><code class="language-python">&lt;question pythonic&gt;
csq_name = "matrix_shape"
csq_prompt = "Enter a 2-by-3 nested list:"
csq_soln = [[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]]

def csq_check_function(submission, solution):
    correct_shape = (
        isinstance(submission, list)
        and len(submission) == 2
        and all(isinstance(row, list) and len(row) == 3
                for row in submission)
    )
    if not correct_shape:
        return 0, """
        &lt;figure&gt;
          &lt;img src="CURRENT/feedback/two-by-three.png"
               alt="A rectangle with two rows and three columns"
               style="max-width: 24rem; width: 100%;"&gt;
          &lt;figcaption&gt;
            The outer list needs two rows, each containing three values.
          &lt;/figcaption&gt;
        &lt;/figure&gt;
        """
    return 1, "Your nested list has the required shape."
&lt;/question&gt;</code></pre>

For this example, place the image beside the page source at:

```text
__STATIC__/feedback/two-by-three.png
```

`CURRENT` is rewritten for feedback just as it is in ordinary page HTML.
Course-wide images can use a `COURSE/...` URL, and an ordinary `https://...`
image URL also works.  Always provide meaningful `alt` text, and remember that
files in `__STATIC__` are publicly accessible.

<catsoop-section>CAT-SOOP's Native Checker Library</catsoop-section>

CAT-SOOP makes its common checker library available in page and preload code
as `csm_check`.  Each function below is a **factory**: call it to obtain the
two-argument function assigned to `csq_check_function`.

| Factory | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `csm_check.equal()` | Uses ordinary Python `submission == solution`. |
| `csm_check.number_close(absolute_threshold=None, ratio_threshold=None)` | Accepts numbers whose difference is within an absolute threshold, a solution-scaled ratio threshold, or the applicable threshold when both are supplied. With neither threshold, it uses equality. |
| `csm_check.list_all(f=None)` | Requires equal-length sequences whose corresponding items pass `f`. The default item checker is `equal()`. |
| `csm_check.list_all_unordered(f=None)` | Compares two collections as unordered lists while preserving duplicate counts. |
| `csm_check.dict_all(f=None)` | Requires identical keys and compares each pair of values with `f`. |
| `csm_check.evaled(f=None)` | Applies `ast.literal_eval` to two text values, then compares the resulting objects with `f`. |
| `csm_check.check_result(f=None)` | Compares the `"result"` fields of two `pythoncode` result records with `f`. It is intended for `pythoncode`, not an ordinary `pythonic` value. |

`equal()` differs slightly from the `pythonic` default: it uses Python
equality without requiring identical types.  Use a custom wrapper when the
distinction between values such as `True` and `1` matters.

`evaled()` is primarily useful when another question type supplies strings
containing Python literals.  A normal raw-mode `pythonic` checker already
receives evaluated Python objects and usually does not need it.

<catsoop-section>Using and Composing Native Checkers</catsoop-section>

Assign a generated checker directly:

<pre><code class="language-python">&lt;question pythonic&gt;
csq_name = "measurement"
csq_prompt = "Enter an estimate of pi:"
csq_soln = 3.141592653589793
csq_check_function = csm_check.number_close(
    absolute_threshold=1e-4
)
&lt;/question&gt;</code></pre>

Factories can be nested.  This accepts an ordered list of numbers, comparing
each item to within \(10^{-6}\):

<pre><code class="language-python">&lt;question pythonic&gt;
csq_name = "approximate_vector"
csq_prompt = "Enter the three computed values:"
csq_soln = [0.25, 0.5, 0.75]
csq_check_function = csm_check.list_all(
    csm_check.number_close(absolute_threshold=1e-6)
)
&lt;/question&gt;</code></pre>

The same technique works for nested structures:

```python
close = csm_check.number_close(absolute_threshold=1e-6)
check_matrix = csm_check.list_all(csm_check.list_all(close))
check_dictionary = csm_check.dict_all(close)
```

Native checkers return Boolean results, so they do not supply their own
messages.  Wrap one when targeted feedback is useful:

<pre><code class="language-python">&lt;question pythonic&gt;
csq_name = "unordered_observations"
csq_prompt = "Enter the observations in any order:"
csq_soln = [1.0, 1.5, 1.5, 2.0]

unordered_close = csm_check.list_all_unordered(
    csm_check.number_close(absolute_threshold=1e-6)
)

def csq_check_function(submission, solution):
    if not isinstance(submission, (list, tuple)):
        return 0, "Enter a list or tuple."
    if len(submission) != len(solution):
        return 0, "Your collection has the wrong number of observations."
    if unordered_close(submission, solution):
        return 1, "All observations are present."
    return 0, (
        "The order may differ, but every value and duplicate "
        "must still be present."
    )
&lt;/question&gt;</code></pre>

<catsoop-section>Where to Define Reusable Checkers</catsoop-section>

A checker used once can live inside its question block.  A checker shared by
several questions on one page can be defined in a page-level
<code>&lt;python&gt;</code> block before those questions.

For a checker used throughout a course, define it in the course's
`preload.py`:

```python
check_close = csm_check.number_close(absolute_threshold=1e-6)
check_close_list = csm_check.list_all(check_close)
```

Descendant pages can then assign `check_close` or `check_close_list` directly.
Use a descriptive name that communicates both the accepted data structure and
the comparison policy.

If a checker grows into a substantial library, move it to a separate Python
file and load it from `preload.py`.  If it also needs a new input interface or
special rendering behavior, it may be time to implement a
[custom question type](COURSE/docs/extending/qtypes) rather than continuing to
expand one check function.

<catsoop-section>Checklist</catsoop-section>

When writing a checker:

* accept `(submission, solution)` even if the solution is not used;
* validate types before applying operations such as `len`, subtraction, or
  iteration;
* return a score from `0` to `1`, with an optional message;
* state partial-credit rules explicitly;
* return focused feedback without revealing the full solution prematurely;
* escape student-controlled values before placing them in HTML;
* add `alt` text to feedback images;
* test correct answers, common mistakes, boundary values, and malformed
  values; and
* keep expensive or reusable domain logic outside the question block.

For the surrounding evaluation and rendering behavior, return to
[The `pythonic` Question Type](CURRENT/..).
